// =====================================================================
//  FluidXPro3: LEXICAL RULES (how source text is read)
// =====================================================================
//  LOCKED. Every other file depends on these rules.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. FILES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  - Extension: .fx3
//  - Encoding: UTF-8 only
//  - One file = one module, named after the file (see modules.fx3)
//  - A file is a list of declarations and statements, read top to bottom


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. COMMENTS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// line comment
# also a line comment (for Python users)
/* block comment,
   can span lines */
/// documentation comment: attaches to the next declaration and is shown
/// by `fx3 doc` and in editor hovers

//  Block comments do NOT nest: /* a /* b */ ends at the first */


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. STATEMENTS & BLOCKS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  - Every statement ends with `;`
//  - A `}` that closes a block does not need `;` after it:
//        if (x > 0) { print(x); }        // no ; after }
//  - EXCEPTION: when a block is used as a value, the statement still
//    needs `;`:
//        string s = if (x > 0) "pos" else "neg";
//        string t = switch(@none; x) { default.act("z"); };
//  - Newlines and indentation have NO meaning. A statement can span
//    many lines.
//  - Blocks always use { }. There are no indentation-based blocks and no
//    brace-less bodies:
//        if (x > 0) print(x);            // error: use { print(x); }
//  - A missing `;` is reported with an exact fix, and `fx3 fmt` inserts it.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. NAMES (identifiers)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  - Start with a letter or `_`. After that: letters, digits, `_`
//  - Unicode letters are allowed, so people can name things in their own
//    language:
//        int गिनती = 0;        float prix = 9.5;
//  - USER-DEFINED names are CASE-SENSITIVE and EXACT:
//        total, Total and TOTAL are three different variables
//  - BUILT-IN vocabulary (types, annotations, keywords, bank methods,
//    argument names) is matched loosely: case, `_` and `-` are ignored.
//        Vector == vector,   @Type_Safe == @typesafe,   modify_at == modifyAt
//  - A user name that would collide with a built-in word after loose
//    matching is allowed, but warns:
//        int Size = 3;   // warning: looks like the built-in `size`
//  - `_` on its own means "ignore this value"
//  - Names may not be keywords (section 5). Wrap a name in backticks to
//    use a keyword anyway:
//        int `repeat` = 3;
//
//  Naming style is not enforced. `fx3 fmt` can suggest one
//  (snake_case variables, PascalCase classes/enums, UPPER_CASE @const).


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. RESERVED KEYWORDS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Declarations   function looper checker pipeline class interface enum
//                 Function Looper Checker Pipeline Class Interface Enum
//  Control        if else repeat for switch case default stop skip return
//                 expose breakpoint branch act
//  Errors         try catch finally fail defer panic ensure
//  Concurrency    async await spawn with
//  Modules        from import as
//  Values         true false yes no on off none this super
//  Operators      and or not in is
//  Other          Action type move @auto @none @unsafe
//
//  Keyword ALIASES (loop, fn, break, continue, throw, ...) are recognised
//  in their position but are NOT reserved. `loop` can still be a
//  variable name.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. LITERALS (full list; variables.fx3 shows them in use)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Integers     42   1_000_000   1'000'000   0xFF   0o17   0b1010
//               suffixes: 10u8  10i32  10u64 ...
//  Floats       3.14   .5   1.5e-3   2.5f (f32)
//  Percent      50%                         -> 0.5
//  Booleans     true false   (also yes no on off, any case)
//  None         none         (also None null NULL nil nullptr nothing)
//  Characters   'a'  '\n'  '\u{1F600}'
//  Strings      "text"  'text'  """multi-line"""  r"raw \n stays"
//               f"Hi {name}"   (see strings.fx3)
//  Durations    500ms  2s  5m  1h  1d       -> duration
//  Sizes        512b  4kb  10mb  2gb        -> int (number of bytes)
//  Collections  [ ]  { }  ( )               (see collections.fx3)
//
//  'x' with ONE character is a char. With more characters it is a string.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7. ANNOTATIONS (grammar)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  @name           no arguments
//  @name(args)     arguments: values, names, expressions
//  - Always written with @
//  - Position is fixed by the construct. For example, in a variable the
//    annotations go between the type and the name.
//  - Several annotations go in any order. Repeating one gives a warning.
//  - Users can't define their own annotations yet. The built-in set is
//    closed (it may open later through a plugin system).
//  - An unknown annotation is treated like an unknown method: alias table,
//    then typo match, then LLM (see compiler.fx3). The answer must be an
//    existing annotation.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 8. WHITESPACE INSIDE TOKENS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  - Multi-word built-in names must be one token: whole_number, not whole number
//  - Operators like `..=`, `~/`, `**`, `??` and `?.` can't contain spaces
//  - [LOCK] `//` ALWAYS starts a comment, even right after a value. Floor division is `~/`
//  - `f"..."`, `r"..."`: no space between the prefix and the quote
